Subject: [PATCH] Windows: size the call-ret shadow-stack guards by allocation granularity

FEX guards the per-thread call/ret shadow stack with FEX_PAGE_SIZE (4 KiB) pages and derives the
HandleAccessViolation() recovery window from the same constant. Both assume the host page size is 4 KiB.

On a 16 KiB-page host (aarch64 Linux under wine) that breaks down. The host rounds the MEM_COMMIT of
CALLRET_STACK_SIZE outwards to its own page size, so the committed r/w region swallows BOTH guard pages:

    reserved  0x7ffffe910000-0x7ffffed11fff   (4 MiB + 2 * 4 KiB)
    committed 0x7ffffe910000-0x7ffffed13fff   <- starts AT the leading guard, ends 12 KiB past the
                                                 trailing one; per /proc/PID/maps neither guard exists

Consequences, both observed with Red Dead Redemption 2, whose return-oriented obfuscator drives this
stack extremely hard:

  * The shadow stack shares host pages with neighbouring allocations, so writes near its ends can touch
    memory that does not belong to it.
  * The first genuinely inaccessible byte is at 0x7ffffed14000, which is ABOVE the AllocationEnd computed
    from FEX_PAGE_SIZE (0x7ffffed12000). HandleAccessViolation() therefore declines, and what should be a
    routine "call-ret stack inbalance" recovery escapes to the guest as a fatal EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION
    that the guest has no handler for.

GetSystemInfo() cannot be used to size the guards: under wine dwPageSize is reported as 4096 to the guest
by design, because x86 guest code assumes 4 KiB pages, regardless of the real host page size.
dwAllocationGranularity is reported honestly (64 KiB) and is >= any page size we are likely to run on, so
use that for both the guard size and the base offset. This also makes the committed region start on a
64 KiB boundary, so it no longer rounds outwards at all. Cost is 128 KiB of reserved (uncommitted) address
space per thread.

With this applied, RDR2 initialises to the point of calling SteamAPI_Init() with zero access violations,
where before it died during startup.

NOTE: FEX explicitly does not support large page sizes, so this may not be upstreamable as-is; it is kept
here so the behaviour and the measurements are written down.
--- a/Source/Windows/Common/CallRetStack.h
+++ b/Source/Windows/Common/CallRetStack.h
@@ -1,12 +1,37 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
 #pragma once
 
+#include <algorithm>
 #include <FEXCore/Core/Context.h>
 #include <FEXCore/Utils/Allocator.h>
 #include <FEXCore/Utils/LogManager.h>
 #include <FEXCore/Debug/InternalThreadState.h>
 
 namespace FEX::Windows::CallRetStack {
+// GUARD SIZING ON A LARGE-PAGE HOST.
+//
+// The guards either side of the shadow stack must be at least one HOST page, and the committed region
+// must start on a host-page boundary — otherwise the host rounds the commit outwards and swallows them.
+// Observed on a 16 KiB-page host (wine on aarch64): with 4 KiB guards, /proc/PID/maps shows the committed
+// r/w region starting AT the leading guard's address and ending 12 KiB past the trailing one, i.e. NEITHER
+// GUARD EXISTS. The shadow stack then silently overruns into the rounding slack and only faults once it
+// leaves the whole reservation — an address outside the window HandleAccessViolation() checks, so the
+// routine "call-ret stack inbalance" recovery declines and the overflow escapes to the guest as a fatal
+// access violation. (RDR2's return-oriented obfuscator overflows this stack constantly, which is how this
+// surfaced.)
+//
+// GetSystemInfo() cannot be used to size this: under wine it reports dwPageSize=4096 to the guest by
+// design (x86 code assumes 4 KiB) regardless of the real host page size. dwAllocationGranularity IS
+// reported honestly (64 KiB) and is >= any page size we might run on, so use that. Cost is 128 KiB of
+// reservation per thread, none of it committed.
+inline uint64_t CallRetGuardSize() {
+  static const uint64_t Size = [] {
+    SYSTEM_INFO Info {};
+    ::GetSystemInfo(&Info);
+    return std::max<uint64_t>(FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE, Info.dwAllocationGranularity);
+  }();
+  return Size;
+}
 struct CallRetStackInfo {
   uint64_t AllocationBase;
   uint64_t AllocationEnd;
@@ -16,21 +41,21 @@
 CallRetStackInfo GetInfoThread(FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState* Thread) {
   uint64_t Base = reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(Thread->CallRetStackBase);
   // Leave some room from the base for the default location to allow for underflows without constant exceptions
-  return {Base - FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE, Base + FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState::CALLRET_STACK_SIZE + FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE,
+  return {Base - CallRetGuardSize(), Base + FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState::CALLRET_STACK_SIZE + CallRetGuardSize(),
           Base + FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState::CALLRET_STACK_SIZE / 4};
 }
 
 void InitializeThread(FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState* Thread) {
   // Allocate the call-ret stack with guard pages on both sides
-  const void* CallRetStackAlloc = ::VirtualAlloc(nullptr, FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState::CALLRET_STACK_SIZE + 2 * FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE,
+  const void* CallRetStackAlloc = ::VirtualAlloc(nullptr, FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState::CALLRET_STACK_SIZE + 2 * CallRetGuardSize(),
                                                  MEM_RESERVE | MEM_TOP_DOWN, PAGE_NOACCESS);
 
   FEXCore::Allocator::VirtualName("FEXMem_CallRetStacks", CallRetStackAlloc,
-                                  FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState::CALLRET_STACK_SIZE + 2 * FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE);
-  FEXCore::Allocator::VirtualTHPControl(CallRetStackAlloc, FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState::CALLRET_STACK_SIZE + 2 * FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE,
+                                  FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState::CALLRET_STACK_SIZE + 2 * CallRetGuardSize());
+  FEXCore::Allocator::VirtualTHPControl(CallRetStackAlloc, FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState::CALLRET_STACK_SIZE + 2 * CallRetGuardSize(),
                                         FEXCore::Allocator::THPControl::Disable);
 
-  Thread->CallRetStackBase = reinterpret_cast<void*>(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(CallRetStackAlloc) + FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE);
+  Thread->CallRetStackBase = reinterpret_cast<void*>(reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(CallRetStackAlloc) + CallRetGuardSize());
   ::VirtualAlloc(Thread->CallRetStackBase, FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState::CALLRET_STACK_SIZE, MEM_COMMIT, PAGE_READWRITE);
 
   Thread->CurrentFrame->State.callret_sp = GetInfoThread(Thread).DefaultLocation;
